ontext | MLflow Team | MLflow (mapping) | Active |\n\n**Component name mappings (non-obvious):** `.context/rhai-components.txt` has no `MLflow Operator`\nor `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` entry; both map to the canonical `MLflow` component (the closest and\nmost-relevant canonical name). Each epic keeps a single canonical component; the strategy-component\nspan is noted here.\n\n**New component detection:** MLflow and MLflow Operator have architecture context files\n(mlflow.md, mlflow-operator.md). mlflow-kubernetes-plugins has no dedicated architecture context\nfile, but it is an existing internal component (v1.5.0, installed into the MLflow image and documented\nin mlflow.md; the strategy's dependency table marks it \"internal, exists\") — it is not a new component\nbeing provisioned, so no repo/konflux onboarding chain is generated. See Health Warnings.\n\n**Passive components (validation folded into ACs, same team):**\n- kube-rbac-proxy — unchanged; deployed as sidecar on the artifacts-only instance identically to the\n  main tracking deployment → AC on the operator epic.\n- Gateway API — existing conditional dependency with runtime CRD discovery → scope of the operator epic.\n- Workspace store (`kubernetes://`) — exists; unreachable-store behavior is fail-closed → ACs on the\n  server epic.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Investigate workspace provider initialization coupling in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Investigate artifact path workspace-context resolvability and enforcement completeness | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Investigate artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Extend mlflow-kubernetes-plugins workspace resolution for the artifacts-only context | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | MLflow operator split-server topology for a dedicated artifacts-only instance | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007 | Tracking server artifact traffic delegation configuration (branch B) | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n\nMain-plan epics: 6 (E001-E006). One conditional branch epic (BRANCH-B-E007) attached to the routing\ninvestigation. Critical path length: 2.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[E001 Investigation: workspace provider init coupling] --> E005[E005 Implementation: plugin workspace resolution]\n    E002[E002 Investigation: path resolvability / enforcement completeness] --> E004[E004 Implementation: workspace-aware artifacts-only serving]\n    E003[E003 Investigation: artifact traffic routing design] --> E006[E006 Implementation: operator split-server topology]\n    E003 -.-> E007B[BRANCH-B-E007 Implementation: server-side delegation config]\n```\n\nArrows point from dependency to dependent. The dotted edge selects a conditional branch epic that is\nnot a main-plan node.\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| E001 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates Implementation) | Open question \"Does the workspace store initialization in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins depend on tracking store availability?\" explicitly \"Determines scope of plugin refactoring needed for the artifacts-only context\" — the answer rewrites the plugin epic's approach (true gate, action=rewrite). |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 3 + Rule 19 | Assumption \"artifact request paths ... include sufficient information to resolve workspace membership\" is flagged \"needs validation\", and risk 1 (artifact path resolution complexity) plus risk 3 (bypass via misconfiguration) leave the enforcement surface unverified. The answer changes the server epic's resolution/validation approach (true gate, action=rewrite). |\n| E003 → E006 | Rule 3 | Open question \"How should the operator configure artifact traffic routing ...?\" explicitly \"Affects HTTPRoute design and determines whether the tracking server needs a configuration change\" — the answer rewrites the operator epic's routing scope (true gate, action=rewrite). |\n| E003 → BRANCH-B-E007 | Rule 3 (conditional branch) | Bounded outcome B (tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations) adds a server-side epic that exists only in that branch. |\n\n**Edges deliberately NOT drawn:**\n\n| Non-edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Server implementation → operator implementation | Rule 13 / Rule 10 analog | The operator deploys the MLflow image by configuration reference, not a build-against artifact. The operator epic carries a \"deployed image supports the combined flags\" AC instead of a DAG edge, so operator development runs in parallel with server development. |\n| Plugin implementation → server implementation | Rules 13/14 | The server delegates to the existing auth plugin interface (`mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point / WorkspaceStoreRegistry), which is unchanged; the plugin epic changes plugin internals behind that interface. Different repos, pre-existing interface → parallel; end-to-end behavior is gated by ACs on both epics. |\n| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (scoping) | The strategy's risk mitigation confines the coupling fix to the plugin (\"factor workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin\"); the server epic's provider interface is stable, so the investigation gates the plugin epic only. |\n| No konflux/repo onboarding chain | Rule 20 (not triggered) | All three components already exist in the platform; no new component is provisioned. |\n\n**Rule 19 handling (safety-critical: tenant isolation / access control):** the strategy introduces new\nauthorization enforcement (workspace validation of artifact requests, fail-closed denial), so Rule 19\napplies. The fail-mode decision itself is already fixed by the strategy (fail-closed is HLR P0-3, NFR\nSecurity, and acceptance criterion 4), so a separate fail-mode investigation would not change what gets\nbuilt (Rule 4). The residual fail-mode unknown (coverage of unresolvable paths, plugin errors, malformed\nworkspace context) and the security unknown (bypass vectors via non-standard artifact roots, risk 3) are\nresolved by the same analysis of the same validation path, so they are combined into E002 per Step 3's\ncombine rule rather than split into two investigations.\n\n**Rule 4 reclassifications (open questions that do NOT change epic structure):**\n\n| Open question / assumption | Handling |\n|---|---|\n| Acceptable latency overhead for workspace validation (PM/Engineering) | AC on the server epic: measure per-request overhead; short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions per the strategy's risk mitigation. Does not change which epics exist. Health warning: no latency target is specified. |\n| Audit telemetry channel for denials (PM/Security) | HLR P2 already commits to server logs; implemented as denial-path logging in the server epic. Channel selection (OTLP / K8s audit log) is a follow-up extension, not a structural change. |\n| Flags composable without breaking individual-flag init sequences | This is the server epic's own deliverable (its ACs cover individual-flag backwards compatibility), not a separate investigation. |\n| Operator Helm chart supports a second Deployment without structural changes | Implementation detail of the operator epic; AC covers rendered resources. |\n| Single-tenant deployments do not set `--enable-workspaces` | Regression AC on the server epic (current behavior preserved when workspace flags absent). |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` + `--workspace-store-uri` | P0 | E004 |\n| Artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | P0 | E004 (enforcement), E005 (resolution capability) |\n| Fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | P0 | E004 (E002 verifies coverage) |\n| Single-tenant / no-workspaces deployments retain current behavior | P0 | E004, E006 |\n| Operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute | P1 | E006 |\n| Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to dedicated instance | P1 | E006, BRANCH-B-E007 (branch B only) |\n| Auth plugin workspace resolution works in artifacts-only context without tracking store init | P1 | E005 |\n| Audit logging for workspace access denials via server logs | P2 | E004 |\n\nAll 8 HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements. The P2 audit-logging HLR stays bundled in E004: it is the\nsame denial code path and shares the same surface as the P0 validation work — splitting it would create\ntwo epics building the same validation path (Step 4 redundancy/bundling rule).\n\n## Priority Inheritance Notes\n\n- E005 is labeled from HLR P1-7 but transitively enables P0 HLR \"artifact requests validated against\n  workspace membership\" → inherits P0.\n- E001 and E002 block P0 work → P0. E003 gates only P1 work → P1.\n- BRANCH-B-E007 enables only P1-6 → P1.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. All dependencies are internal and existing (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API,\nkube-rbac-proxy); there is no external dependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1/Tier 2 split.\n\n## Conditional Branches\n\nThe routing-design investigation (E003) has two bounded outcomes:\n\n- **Outcome A — HTTPRoute-only routing**: Gateway API HTTPRoute rules route artifact traffic from the\n  main tracking deployment's surface to the dedicated artifacts-only instance; no tracking server code\n  change. Main-plan E006 covers this alone; no branch epic.\n- **Outcome B — server-side delegation required**: the tracking server needs a configuration change to\n  delegate artifact operations to the dedicated instance. BRANCH-B-E007 implements the server-side\n  delegation configuration; it is attached to the investigation as a plan document, not created unless\n  outcome B holds.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Priority label vs. enablement**: the strategy labels the plugin resolution work P1 (HLR), but P0\n   workspace validation cannot function without it; priority inheritance raises the plugin epic to P0.\n2. **No latency target**: the NFR admits no latency target is specified; the caching requirement\n   (launch vs. follow-up) is an open PM/Engineering decision. Mitigated by an AC to measure overhead\n   and implement short-TTL caching consistent with existing auth-path caching.\n3. **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins lacks a dedicated architecture context file**: the plugin epic works\n   from mlflow.md's description of the entry-point interface; the source repo location should be\n   confirmed at epic start (repo_access scored neutral).\n4. **Process gates pending**: Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation and other\n   prerequisites are \"Pending human review\" at strategy level; they are not modeled as epics.\n5. **Non-standard artifact roots**: initial implementation is scoped to the S3 artifact store\n   (`{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`); limitations for custom artifact roots must be documented\n   (risk 1 mitigation) — a documented restriction, not a scope expansion.\n6. **Misconfiguration risk**: a split topology adds operational surface; the operator epic must reject\n   split-serving configurations whose artifacts-only instance lacks workspace flags (risk 3 mitigation).\n```\n\nHmm wait — in the summary, cross-references to epic IDs are fine (only epic BODIES can't reference siblings). Good.\n\nNow epic bodies.\n\n### E001 body\n\n```markdown\n# Investigate workspace provider initialization coupling in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\n\n## Description\n\nResolve whether the Kubernetes workspace provider in the `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` package can\ninitialize and resolve experiment-to-workspace mapping in a server context where the tracking store\nand model registry store are not initialized. The strategy flags this as an open question\n(\"Does the workspace store initialization in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins depend on tracking store\navailability?\") and as an assumption needing validation (\"the workspace store provides\nexperiment-to-workspace mapping that is accessible without initializing tracking stores\"). The answer\ndetermines whether the plugin's workspace resolution must be refactored into an independent\ninitialization path before it can back workspace-aware artifacts-only serving.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Trace the plugin's initialization dependency chain: registration through the\n  `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point group, loading via `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`, and the\n  `kubernetes://` workspace store resolution path.\n- Determine definitively whether any part of that chain requires tracking store or model registry\n  store initialization.\n- If coupled: produce a refactoring plan that factors workspace resolution into an independent\n  initialization path within the plugin, preserving the existing entry-point interface and the\n  plugin's behavior for the tracking server's non-artifact operations.\n- If not coupled: produce verification evidence (e.g., a reproducible test exercising\n  experiment-to-workspace resolution with tracking stores uninitialized).\n- Record the bounded outcome (coupled / not coupled) and the resulting scope for the plugin\n  extension work.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A findings document states, with code references, whether the workspace provider's initialization\n  and resolution path depends on tracking store or model registry store availability, and identifies\n  the exact coupling points if it does.\n- If coupled, the refactoring plan scopes the independent initialization path, lists the affected\n  plugin modules, and confirms the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry-point interface is preserved.\n- If not coupled, a reproducible verification demonstrates experiment-to-workspace resolution\n  succeeding with tracking and model registry stores uninitialized.\n- The outcome is recorded as one of the two bounded answers and is sufficient to set the scope of\n  the downstream plugin extension work without further analysis.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Resolves the open question and assumption gating HLR \"Auth plugin workspace resolution functions\n  correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization\" (P1) and, through\n  it, HLR \"Artifact requests ... are validated against workspace membership\" (P0).\n- Addresses risk \"Auth plugin coupling to full server initialization\" (validate the plugin's\n  initialization dependencies early, per the strategy's mitigation).\n```\n\n### E002 body\n\n```markdown\n# Investigate artifact path workspace-context resolvability and enforcement completeness\n\n## Description\n\nResolve whether artifact requests in the artifacts-only server carry enough information to resolve\nworkspace membership, and whether the planned workspace validation closes every failure and bypass\nmode. The strategy flags the assumption \"artifact request paths in the `/api/2.0/mlflow/artifacts/*`\nendpoints include sufficient information (experiment ID or run ID) to resolve workspace membership\"\nas needing validation, and its risks identify artifact path resolution complexity and the possibility\nof artifact requests bypassing workspace validation. Because this strategy adds new authorization\nenforcement (tenant isolation with fail-closed denial), this investigation provides the fail-mode and\nsecurity analysis that gates the server implementation.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Determine, for the S3 artifact store configuration (the standard RHOAI configuration, artifact\n  paths following `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), whether every artifact request path\n  resolves to a parent experiment and thus to a workspace assignment.\n- Identify artifact request shapes that cannot be resolved (non-standard artifact roots, custom\n  artifact root configurations, malformed paths) and confirm each is denied under the fail-closed\n  design rather than served.\n- Enumerate failure modes of the validation path — workspace store unreachable, workspace store\n  error vs. definitive non-membership, auth plugin failure, invalid or missing workspace context —\n  and verify the fail-closed design (deny, never serve without validation) covers each.\n- Identify bypass vectors for workspace validation in artifact serving (path forms that evade\n  experiment resolution, direct access paths that skip validation) and classify each as covered,\n  denied by design, or requiring remediation.\n- Produce recommendations that fix the resolution and validation approach for the workspace-aware\n  artifacts-only server, including any remediation work discovered.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A findings document answers, with path-structure and code evidence, whether S3 artifact request\n  paths carry sufficient information to resolve workspace membership, and specifies the resolution\n  rule the server implementation should apply.\n- Every enumerated failure mode and bypass vector is classified as deny-by-design (fail-closed),\n  covered by existing validation, or flagged with concrete remediation for the server implementation.\n- No mode is left classified as \"serve without validation\"; any uncovered mode is escalated as\n  remediation scope for the workspace-aware artifacts-only serving implementation.\n- Documented limitations for non-standard artifact roots are drafted (per the strategy's risk\n  mitigation) so the server implementation can include them.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Validates the assumption gating HLR \"`--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces`\n  ...\" (P0) and HLR \"Artifact requests ... are validated against workspace membership; requests from\n  users without workspace membership are denied with HTTP 403\" (P0).\n- Verifies fail-closed coverage for HLR \"Workspace-aware artifact serving is fail-closed: if the\n  workspace store is unreachable, requests are denied\" (P0) and the Security NFR (\"Requests with\n  invalid or missing workspace context are denied, not served\").\n- Addresses risks \"Artifact path resolution complexity\" and \"Split-deployment operational complexity\"\n  (bypass via misconfiguration).\n```\n\n### E003 body\n\n```markdown\n# Investigate artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology\n\n## Description\n\nDecide how the MLflow operator should configure artifact traffic routing from the main tracking\ndeployment to the dedicated artifacts-only instance in the split-server topology. The strategy flags\nthis as an open question owned by the MLflow Team: the answer \"affects HTTPRoute design and\ndetermines whether the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact\noperations\". The answer selects between two bounded outcomes and sets the routing scope of the\noperator's split-server implementation (and, in one outcome, adds server-side delegation work).\n\n## Scope\n\n- Analyze the operator's existing Gateway API routing pattern (conditional HTTPRoute creation via\n  runtime CRD discovery) for its applicability to routing artifact traffic to a dedicated\n  artifacts-only instance.\n- Determine whether HTTPRoute-based routing alone can direct artifact API traffic\n  (`/api/2.0/mlflow/artifacts/*` and the artifact proxy surface) from the main tracking deployment's\n  route to the artifacts-only instance's Service.\n- Determine whether the tracking server instead needs a configuration change to delegate artifact\n  operations to the dedicated instance (e.g., an upstream/proxy configuration on the main tracking\n  deployment).\n- Assess backwards-compatibility impact: deployments not using split-server artifact serving must\n  reconcile unchanged.\n- Record the bounded outcome: (A) HTTPRoute-only routing suffices, or (B) tracking server\n  configuration change required, with the design each outcome implies.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A design document selects the routing mechanism with rationale grounded in the operator's existing\n  Helm-in-operator and conditional CRD discovery patterns, and states which bounded outcome holds.\n- For the selected outcome, the design specifies what the operator renders/configures for artifact\n  traffic routing, sufficient to set the scope of the split-server operator implementation without\n  further design work.\n- If outcome B holds, the design specifies the tracking-server-side configuration change needed to\n  delegate artifact operations, sufficient to scope a server-side delegation implementation.\n- The design confirms deployments not opting into split-server artifact serving are unaffected.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Resolves the open question gating HLR \"Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the\n  dedicated artifacts-only instance\" (P1) and informing HLR \"MLflow operator deploys a dedicated\n  artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute\" (P1).\n```\n\n### E004 body\n\n```markdown\n# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n\n## Description\n\nMake `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` in the MLflow tracking server so the\nartifacts-only server initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests\nagainst tenant boundaries. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit\n`--workspace-store-uri`, resolves each artifact request's path to its parent experiment and the\nexperiment's workspace assignment, and delegates authorization to the existing auth plugin\ninfrastructure. No new server flag is introduced (Staff Engineer input: the workspace-aware behavior\nemerges from combining existing flags). Tracking stores and model registry stores stay\nuninitialized; non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Flag composition: allow `--artifacts-only` to combine with `--enable-workspaces`; require an\n  explicit `--workspace-store-uri` in combined mode and fail startup with a clear error when it is\n  missing.\n- Initialize only the workspace provider needed for artifact validation, without tracking store or\n  model registry store initialization; keep non-artifact API endpoints disabled.\n- Workspace validation of artifact requests: resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment\n  (S3 artifact store paths following `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), look up the\n  experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the\n  existing auth plugin infrastructure — without prescribing Kubernetes API mechanics inside the\n  plugin (Staff Engineer input: SSAR detail is an implementation detail of the plugin, not the\n  server).\n- Fail-closed behavior: if the workspace-aware mode is active but the workspace store is\n  unreachable, deny artifact requests (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable)\n  rather than serving without validation; requests with invalid, missing, or unresolvable workspace\n  context are denied, not served.\n- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` retains current\n  behavior (artifacts served without workspace validation); each flag used individually preserves\n  its current initialization sequence; no existing CLI arguments or configuration keys change\n  meaning.\n- Audit logging: workspace access denials in artifact serving are logged through server logs.\n- Performance: measure per-request overhead of workspace resolution; implement short-TTL caching of\n  workspace membership decisions consistent with caching patterns already used in the tracking\n  server's auth path (the strategy sets no latency target — see open question owned by\n  PM/Engineering).\n- Document limitations for non-standard artifact roots (initial implementation scoped to the S3\n  artifact store per the strategy's risk mitigation).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given the server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri\n  kubernetes://`, a user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact belonging to an\n  experiment in workspace A receives HTTP 200 with the correct artifact content.\n- Given the same deployment, a user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an\n  artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B receives HTTP 403 with an error message\n  indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n- Given the server started with `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces`, any authenticated\n  user's artifact request is served without workspace validation (HTTP 200 regardless of workspace\n  membership) — current behavior preserved.\n- Given the workspace-aware artifacts-only server running with the workspace store (Kubernetes API)\n  unreachable, artifact requests are denied with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is\n  unavailable (fail-closed).\n- Artifact requests with invalid, missing, or unresolvable workspace context are denied, not served.\n- Starting the server with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` but no `--workspace-store-uri`\n  fails with a clear error message.\n- In combined mode, tracking stores and model registry stores remain uninitialized and non-artifact\n  API endpoints remain disabled.\n- Workspace access denials in artifact serving appear in server logs.\n- Per-request latency overhead of workspace resolution is measured and reported; short-TTL caching\n  of workspace membership decisions is implemented consistent with the tracking server's existing\n  auth-path caching.\n- Existing behavior for each flag used individually is unchanged (regression: single-flag and\n  no-flag startup sequences and artifact serving behave as before).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- P0: `--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` to\n  enable workspace-validated artifact serving.\n- P0: Artifact requests through the workspace-aware artifacts-only server are validated against\n  workspace membership; requests from users without workspace membership are denied with HTTP 403.\n- P0: Workspace-aware artifact serving is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable,\n  requests are denied.\n- P0: Single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current\n  behavior with no changes.\n- P2: Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs (bundled:\n  same denial code path and surface as the P0 validation work — not deferrable as an independent\n  feature).\n```\n\nWait — scope must not reference sibling epics. \"see open question owned by PM/Engineering\" fine. No E00x references. Good. Also should not say \"the workspace-aware artifacts-only serving implementation\" referencing other epic — that was in E002's AC (\"escalated as remediation scope for the workspace-aware artifacts-only serving implementation\") — that's describing capability in plain terms, allowed (\"the OTEL env var injection capability\" style). OK.\n\n### E005 body\n\n```markdown\n# Extend mlflow-kubernetes-plugins workspace resolution for the artifacts-only context\n\n## Description\n\nVerify and extend the `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` auth plugin so workspace resolution functions\ncorrectly in the artifacts-only server context, where tracking stores and model registry stores are\nnot initialized. The plugin already provides experiment-to-workspace mapping for the tracking\nserver's non-artifact operations through the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point group; this\nepic ensures the same resolution works when only the workspace provider is initialized. The\nauthentication layer is unchanged: the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth`\nsetup work identically for the artifacts-only instance and the main tracking server (Staff Engineer\ninput).\n\n## Scope\n\n- Verify workspace resolution (experiment-to-workspace mapping via the `kubernetes://` workspace\n  store) in a server context with tracking and model registry stores uninitialized.\n- If the workspace provider's initialization is coupled to tracking store availability (per the\n  findings of the workspace provider initialization investigation): refactor workspace resolution\n  into an independent initialization path within the plugin, preserving the\n  `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry-point interface and `WorkspaceStoreRegistry` loading.\n- Extend the plugin as needed so artifact request paths can be resolved to workspace membership\n  through the same resolution logic the tracking server uses for non-artifact operations.\n- Preserve existing behavior for the tracking server's non-artifact workspace operations (no\n  regression).\n- Do not change the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar or `--app-name kubernetes-auth` authentication setup.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Workspace resolution returns the correct experiment-to-workspace mapping in the artifacts-only\n  context, demonstrated with tracking and model registry stores uninitialized (HLR: auth plugin\n  workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store\n  initialization).\n- If refactoring was required: workspace resolution initializes through an independent path, the\n  `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry-point interface is unchanged, and the plugin still loads via\n  `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`.\n- The plugin's behavior for the tracking server's existing non-artifact workspace operations is\n  unchanged (regression tests pass).\n- The authentication setup (kube-rbac-proxy sidecar, `--app-name kubernetes-auth`) requires no\n  changes and works identically for the artifacts-only instance.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- P1 (inherited P0): Auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only\n  context without tracking store initialization — enables the P0 workspace-membership validation of\n  artifact requests.\n- Addresses dependency \"mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin (internal, exists): may require\n  extension if workspace resolution is currently coupled to the full tracking server initialization\n  path\" and risk \"Auth plugin coupling to full server initialization\".\n```\n\n### E006 body\n\n```markdown\n# MLflow operator split-server topology for a dedicated artifacts-only instance\n\n## Description\n\nExtend the MLflow operator to deploy a dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance as a separate\nDeployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute, and to configure the main tracking deployment to\nroute artifact traffic to that instance — a split-server topology, not a single-deployment\nconfiguration toggle (Staff Engineer input). The operator continues to use its Helm-in-operator\npattern: MLflow CR spec converted to Helm values, rendered through embedded chart templates, applied\nvia Server-Side Apply, with Gateway API HTTPRoute resources created conditionally based on runtime\nCRD discovery.\n\n## Scope\n\n- New Helm chart templates for the artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute, separate from\n  the main tracking Deployment's resources.\n- Propagation of workspace configuration (`--enable-workspaces`, `--workspace-store-uri`,\n  `--app-name kubernetes-auth`) to the artifacts-only instance using the same configuration\n  mechanisms already used for the main tracking deployment.\n- Configuration of artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated\n  artifacts-only instance, per the mechanism selected by the artifact traffic routing design\n  investigation (HTTPRoute-based routing, or routing that relies on a tracking-server-side\n  delegation configuration if that outcome holds).\n- Declarative CR surface: users configure a single MLflow CR for the split topology rather than\n  managing individual resources.\n- Operator validation that rejects split-serving configurations where the artifacts-only instance\n  lacks workspace flags (misconfiguration could let artifact requests bypass workspace validation —\n  strategy risk mitigation).\n- kube-rbac-proxy sidecar deployed on the artifacts-only instance identically to the main tracking\n  deployment (passive component: authentication layer unchanged).\n- Backwards compatibility: MLflow CRs not using split-server artifact serving reconcile exactly as\n  before.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given an MLflow CR with split-server artifact serving enabled, reconciliation creates a dedicated\n  artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute separate from the main tracking Deployment —\n  measured by the presence of distinct Deployment resources with the correct flag combinations.\n- The artifacts-only Deployment's container command runs with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces\n  --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts` — measured by\n  inspecting the container command in the rendered Deployment spec.\n- The MLflow image deployed to the artifacts-only instance supports the combined\n  `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` serving mode (configuration reference gate — the image\n  digest is resolved at deployment time, not a build-time dependency).\n- Artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated artifacts-only\n  instance is configured per the selected routing design.\n- HTTPRoute resources for the artifacts-only instance are created conditionally based on runtime CRD\n  discovery, consistent with the operator's existing pattern for optional dependencies.\n- The operator rejects (with a clear status condition) split-serving configurations whose\n  artifacts-only instance lacks workspace flags.\n- The artifacts-only Deployment includes the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar configured identically to the\n  main tracking deployment.\n- MLflow CRs without split-server artifact serving reconcile with no changes to previously rendered\n  resources (backwards compatibility).\n- The split topology is expressed declaratively through a single MLflow CR.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- P1: MLflow operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its\n  own Service and HTTPRoute.\n- P1: Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n- P0 (regression share): Single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces`\n  retain current behavior with no changes.\n```\n\n### BRANCH-B-E007 body\n\n```markdown\n# Tracking server artifact traffic delegation configuration (branch B)\n\n## Description\n\nImplement the tracking-server-side configuration change that delegates artifact operations to the\ndedicated artifacts-only instance in the split-server topology. This epic exists only if the\nartifact traffic routing design investigation resolves to the outcome where HTTPRoute-based routing\nalone is insufficient and the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact\noperations. If HTTPRoute-only routing suffices, this epic is not needed.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Implement the tracking server configuration mechanism that routes/delegates artifact operations\n  to the dedicated artifacts-only instance, per the selected routing design.\n- Ensure the configuration is settable through the mechanisms the MLflow operator already uses to\n  configure the main tracking deployment, so the operator can propagate it declaratively from the\n  MLflow CR.\n- Preserve current behavior when the delegation configuration is absent: the main tracking server\n  continues to serve artifacts itself.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- With the delegation configuration set, artifact requests to the main tracking server are\n  routed to the dedicated artifacts-only instance (measured by artifact responses originating from\n  the artifacts-only instance).\n- Without the delegation configuration, the main tracking server serves artifacts itself — current\n  behavior preserved (backwards compatibility).\n- The configuration can be propagated by the MLflow operator through its existing configuration\n  mechanisms for the main tracking deployment.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- P1: Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance\n  (branch-B share of this HLR, alongside the operator's split-server topology implementation).\n```\n\nNow signal files. Format: markdown table with Signal, Value, Rationale columns.\n\nE001 signals (investigation):\n- question_specificity: 1 — concrete question (does workspace provider init depend on tracking store availability) with a defined pass/fail (resolution succeeds/fails without tracking stores).\n- source_accessibility: 1 — the plugin source (internal package, v1.5.0, also on PyPI) and the MLflow server's WorkspaceStoreRegistry loading code contain the answer.\n- local_runnability: 1 — Python package exercisable locally; the provider's initialization path can be exercised with a pip-installed plugin and a stubbed Kubernetes client.\n- cluster_hardware_dependence: 0 — the gating question is answerable from source and local execution with mocks; no live cluster, GPU, or prod-scale needed.\n- human_judgment_required: 0 — technical question owned by the MLflow Team; no governance or external-party decision.\n\nE002 signals:\n- question_specificity: 1 — enumerable questions (does the S3 path structure resolve to a workspace; which failure/bypass modes exist) with defined criterion (every mode classified deny-by-design, covered, or remediation).\n- source_accessibility: 1 — MLflow server source (artifact proxy, artifacts-only mode) and mlflow.md architecture context hold the path-structure and endpoint facts.\n- local_runnability: 1 — the artifacts-only server is a pip-installable Python service