{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-ab3fac82-232b-411e-91d7-32a1abae43c2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-86510cf7038bacfb","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-8nh9oix9/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8009 — Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n\n## Triage\n\nFull decomposition (not triaged to a single epic). The strategy is M-L effort (4-7 sprints), spans\nthree affected components (MLflow, MLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins) with code changes in\neach, and carries strategy-flagged open questions and \"needs validation\" assumptions whose answers\nchange downstream epic scope. Not docs-only: every affected component requires code changes.\n\n## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n\nAll three Staff Engineer / SME corrections are incorporated; the strategy's technical approach already\nreflects them, and epic scope follows the corrected direction:\n\n| # | Staff Engineer Input | Effect on decomposition |\n|---|---------------------|-------------------------|\n| 1 | No new flag — combine `--artifacts-only` with the existing `--enable-workspaces` | No epic creates `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` or `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware`; the server implementation epic composes existing flags only |\n| 2 | Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle | The operator epic is scoped as a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute with artifact traffic routing, not a CR-field toggle |\n| 3 | SSAR detail is over-specified (plugin implementation detail, not strategy-level) | Epic scope states that authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure without dictating Kubernetes API mechanics; kube-rbac-proxy / `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup unchanged |\n\n## Component Graph\n\n| Strategy component | Change | Owner team | Canonical Jira component | Active/Passive |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| MLflow | `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces`; workspace provider init without tracking/registry stores; workspace validation of artifact requests | MLflow Team | MLflow | Active |\n| MLflow Operator | Dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute; artifact traffic routing from main tracking deployment | MLflow Team | MLflow (mapping) | Active |\n| mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | Verify and extend workspace resolution for the artifacts-only context | MLflow Team | MLflow (mapping) | Active |\n\n**Component name mappings (non-obvious):** `.context/rhai-components.txt` has no `MLflow Operator`\nor `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` entry. Both map to the canonical `MLflow` component — the closest,\nmost-relevant canonical name (submit.py strips non-canonical values, leaving the Jira epic\ncomponent-less). Each epic carries a single canonical component; the strategy-component span is\nnoted here.\n\n**New component detection:** MLflow and MLflow Operator have architecture context files (mlflow.md,\nmlflow-operator.md). mlflow-kubernetes-plugins has no dedicated architecture-context file, but it is\nan existing internal component (v1.5.0, installed into the MLflow image and documented in mlflow.md;\nthe strategy's dependency table marks it \"internal, exists\") — it is not a new component being\nprovisioned, so no Rule 20 repo/konflux onboarding chain is generated. See Health Warnings.\n\n**Passive components (validation folded into ACs, same team):**\n\n- **kube-rbac-proxy** — unchanged; deployed as a sidecar on the artifacts-only instance identically\n  to the main tracking deployment → AC on the operator epic.\n- **Gateway API** — existing conditional dependency with runtime CRD discovery → scope of the\n  operator epic.\n- **Workspace store (`kubernetes://`)** — exists; unreachable-store behavior is fail-closed → ACs on\n  the server epic.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Investigate workspace provider initialization coupling in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Investigate artifact path workspace-context resolvability and enforcement completeness | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Investigate artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Extend mlflow-kubernetes-plugins workspace resolution for the artifacts-only context | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | MLflow operator split-server topology for a dedicated artifacts-only instance | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007 | Tracking server artifact traffic delegation configuration (branch B) | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n\nMain-plan epics: 6 (E001-E006). One conditional branch epic (BRANCH-B-E007) attached to the routing\ninvestigation. Critical path length: 2.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[E001 Investigation: workspace provider init coupling] --> E005[E005 Implementation: plugin workspace resolution]\n    E002[E002 Investigation: path resolvability / enforcement completeness] --> E004[E004 Implementation: workspace-aware artifacts-only serving]\n    E003[E003 Investigation: artifact traffic routing design] --> E006[E006 Implementation: operator split-server topology]\n    E003 -.-> E007B[BRANCH-B-E007 Implementation: server-side delegation config]\n```\n\nArrows point from dependency to dependent. The dotted edge selects a conditional branch epic, which\nis not a main-plan node. All three investigations are parallel roots; the three implementations are\nparallel once their gating investigation resolves.\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| E001 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates Implementation) | Open question \"Does the workspace store initialization in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins depend on tracking store availability?\" explicitly \"Determines scope of plugin refactoring needed for the artifacts-only context\" — the answer rewrites the plugin epic's approach (true gate, `rewrite`). |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 3 + Rule 19 | The assumption \"artifact request paths ... include sufficient information to resolve workspace membership\" is flagged \"needs validation\", and risks 1 (path resolution complexity) and 3 (bypass via misconfiguration) leave the enforcement surface unverified. The answer changes the server epic's resolution/validation approach (true gate, `rewrite`). |\n| E003 → E006 | Rule 3 | Open question \"How should the operator configure artifact traffic routing ...?\" explicitly \"Affects HTTPRoute design and determines whether the tracking server needs a configuration change\" — the answer rewrites the operator epic's routing scope (true gate, `rewrite`). |\n| E003 → BRANCH-B-E007 | Rule 3 (conditional branch) | Bounded outcome B (tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations) adds a server-side epic that exists only in that branch. |\n\n**Rule 19 handling (safety-critical: tenant isolation / access control).** The strategy introduces new\nauthorization enforcement (workspace validation of artifact requests with fail-closed denial), so\nRule 19 applies. The fail-mode decision itself, however, is already fixed by the strategy (fail-closed\nis HLR P0-3, the Security NFR, and acceptance criterion 4), so a standalone fail-mode investigation\nwould not change what gets built (Rule 4). The residual fail-mode unknown (coverage of unresolvable\npaths, plugin errors, malformed workspace context) and the security unknown (bypass vectors via\nnon-standard artifact roots, misconfiguration) are resolved by the same analysis of the same\nvalidation path, so they are combined into E002 per Step 3's combine rule rather than split into two\ninvestigations.\n\n**Edges deliberately NOT drawn:**\n\n| Non-edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Server implementation → operator implementation | Rule 13 (configuration reference) | The operator deploys the MLflow image by configuration reference, not a build-against artifact. The operator epic carries a \"deployed image supports the combined flags\" AC instead of a DAG edge, so operator and server development run in parallel. |\n| Plugin implementation → server implementation | Rules 13/14 | The server delegates to the existing auth plugin interface (`mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point / `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`), which is unchanged; the plugin epic changes plugin internals behind that interface. Different repos, pre-existing interface → parallel; end-to-end behavior is gated by ACs on both epics. |\n| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (scoping) | The strategy's risk mitigation confines the coupling fix to the plugin (\"factor workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin\"); the server epic's provider interface is stable, so the investigation gates the plugin epic only. |\n| No konflux/repo onboarding chain | Rule 20 (not triggered) | All three components already exist in the platform; no new component is provisioned. |\n\n**Rule 4 reclassifications (unknowns that do NOT change epic structure):**\n\n| Open question / assumption | Handling |\n|---|---|\n| Acceptable latency overhead for workspace validation (PM/Engineering) | AC on the server epic: measure per-request overhead; implement short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions per the strategy's risk mitigation. Does not change which epics exist. Health warning: no latency target is specified. |\n| Audit telemetry channel for denials (PM/Security) | HLR P2 already commits to server logs; implemented as denial-path logging in the server epic. Channel selection (OTLP / Kubernetes audit log) is a follow-up extension, not a structural change. |\n| Flags composable without breaking individual-flag init sequences | This is the server epic's own deliverable (its ACs cover individual-flag backwards compatibility), not a separate investigation. |\n| Operator Helm chart supports a second Deployment without structural changes | Implementation detail of the operator epic; AC covers the rendered resources. |\n| Single-tenant deployments do not set `--enable-workspaces` | Regression AC on the server epic (current behavior preserved when workspace flags are absent). |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` to enable workspace-validated artifact serving | P0 | E004 |\n| Artifact requests through the workspace-aware artifacts-only server are validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | P0 | E004 (enforcement), E005 (resolution capability) |\n| Workspace-aware artifact serving is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable, requests are denied | P0 | E004 (E002 verifies coverage) |\n| Single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior with no changes | P0 | E004, E006 |\n| MLflow operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute | P1 | E006 |\n| Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance | P1 | E006, BRANCH-B-E007 (branch B only) |\n| Auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization | P1 | E005 |\n| Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs | P2 | E004 |\n\nAll 8 HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements. The P2 audit-logging HLR stays bundled in E004: it is\nthe same denial code path and shares the same surface as the P0 validation work — a deferral split\nwould create two epics building the same validation path (Step 4 bundling rule for tightly coupled\nlower-priority HLRs).\n\n## Priority Inheritance Notes\n\n- E005 maps to a P1 HLR but transitively enables the P0 workspace-membership validation HLR →\n  inherits P0.\n- E001 and E002 block P0 work → P0. E003 gates only P1 work → P1.\n- BRANCH-B-E007 enables only the P1 artifact-traffic-routing HLR → P1.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. All dependencies are internal and existing (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway\nAPI, kube-rbac-proxy); there is no external dependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1/Tier 2\nsplit is needed.\n\n## Conditional Branches\n\nThe routing-design investigation (E003) has two bounded outcomes:\n\n- **Outcome A — HTTPRoute-only routing suffices**: Gateway API HTTPRoute rules direct artifact\n  traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance; no tracking server code change. Main-plan E006\n  covers this alone; no branch epic exists.\n- **Outcome B — server-side delegation required**: the tracking server needs a configuration change\n  to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated instance. BRANCH-B-E007 implements the\n  server-side delegation configuration; it is attached to the investigation as a plan document and\n  is created only if outcome B holds.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Priority label vs. enablement**: the strategy labels the plugin resolution work P1 (HLR), but\n   the P0 workspace-validation HLR cannot function without it; priority inheritance raises the\n   plugin epic to P0.\n2. **No latency target**: the Performance NFR admits no latency target is specified; whether caching\n   is required at launch is an open PM/Engineering decision. Mitigated by an AC to measure overhead\n   and implement short-TTL caching consistent with existing auth-path caching.\n3. **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins lacks a dedicated architecture-context file**: the plugin epic works\n   from mlflow.md's description of the entry-point interface; the source repo location should be\n   confirmed at epic start (repo_access scored neutral).\n4. **Strategy-level process gates pending**: Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, Licence\n   Validation and other prerequisites are \"Pending human review\"; they are strategy-level gates and\n   are not modeled as epics.\n5. **Non-standard artifact roots**: the initial implementation is scoped to the S3 artifact store\n   (`{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`); limitations for custom artifact roots must be documented\n   (risk 1 mitigation) — a documented restriction, not a scope expansion.\n6. **Split-topology misconfiguration risk**: a split server adds operational surface where\n   misconfiguration could let artifact requests bypass workspace validation; the operator epic must\n   reject split-serving configurations whose artifacts-only instance lacks workspace flags\n   (risk 3 mitigation).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":64932,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88a7f54f0bdff0ba","agent_id":"ac3ea9291bd90e561","session_id":"7838be6a-6dd6-47c0-b294-a71fb9a59c49","uuid":"4b680028-2190-4aca-b8db-74d87948aa8b","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:19:44.325Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009 into epics"}
