{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8fb6ade9e039bbef","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8009 — Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n11\t\n12\t## Triage\n13\t\n14\tFull decomposition (not triaged to a single epic). The strategy is M-L effort (4-7 sprints), spans\n15\tthree affected components (MLflow, MLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins) with code changes in\n16\teach, and carries strategy-flagged open questions and \"needs validation\" assumptions whose answers\n17\tchange downstream epic scope. Not docs-only: every affected component requires code changes.\n18\t\n19\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n20\t\n21\tAll three Staff Engineer / SME corrections are incorporated; the strategy's technical approach already\n22\treflects them, and epic scope follows the corrected direction:\n23\t\n24\t| # | Staff Engineer Input | Effect on decomposition |\n25\t|---|---------------------|-------------------------|\n26\t| 1 | No new flag — combine `--artifacts-only` with the existing `--enable-workspaces` | No epic creates `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` or `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware`; the server implementation epic composes existing flags only |\n27\t| 2 | Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle | The operator epic is scoped as a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute with artifact traffic routing, not a CR-field toggle |\n28\t| 3 | SSAR detail is over-specified (plugin implementation detail, not strategy-level) | Epic scope states that authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure without dictating Kubernetes API mechanics; kube-rbac-proxy / `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup unchanged |\n29\t\n30\t## Component Graph\n31\t\n32\t| Strategy component | Change | Owner team | Canonical Jira component | Active/Passive |\n33\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n34\t| MLflow | `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces`; workspace provider init without tracking/registry stores; workspace validation of artifact requests | MLflow Team | MLflow | Active |\n35\t| MLflow Operator | Dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute; artifact traffic routing from main tracking deployment | MLflow Team | MLflow (mapping) | Active |\n36\t| mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | Verify and extend workspace resolution for the artifacts-only context | MLflow Team | MLflow (mapping) | Active |\n37\t\n38\t**Component name mappings (non-obvious):** `.context/rhai-components.txt` has no `MLflow Operator`\n39\tor `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` entry. Both map to the canonical `MLflow` component — the closest,\n40\tmost-relevant canonical name (submit.py strips non-canonical values, leaving the Jira epic\n41\tcomponent-less). Each epic carries a single canonical component; the strategy-component span is\n42\tnoted here.\n43\t\n44\t**New component detection:** MLflow and MLflow Operator have architecture context files (mlflow.md,\n45\tmlflow-operator.md). mlflow-kubernetes-plugins has no dedicated architecture-context file, but it is\n46\tan existing internal component (v1.5.0, installed into the MLflow image and documented in mlflow.md;\n47\tthe strategy's dependency table marks it \"internal, exists\") — it is not a new component being\n48\tprovisioned, so no Rule 20 repo/konflux onboarding chain is generated. See Health Warnings.\n49\t\n50\t**Passive components (validation folded into ACs, same team):**\n51\t\n52\t- **kube-rbac-proxy** — unchanged; deployed as a sidecar on the artifacts-only instance identically\n53\t  to the main tracking deployment → AC on the operator epic.\n54\t- **Gateway API** — existing conditional dependency with runtime CRD discovery → scope of the\n55\t  operator epic.\n56\t- **Workspace store (`kubernetes://`)** — exists; unreachable-store behavior is fail-closed → ACs on\n57\t  the server epic.\n58\t\n59\t## Epic List\n60\t\n61\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n62\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n63\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Investigate workspace provider initialization coupling in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n64\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Investigate artifact path workspace-context resolvability and enforcement completeness | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n65\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Investigate artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n66\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n67\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Extend mlflow-kubernetes-plugins workspace resolution for the artifacts-only context | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n68\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | MLflow operator split-server topology for a dedicated artifacts-only instance | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n69\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007 | Tracking server artifact traffic delegation configuration (branch B) | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n70\t\n71\tMain-plan epics: 6 (E001-E006). One conditional branch epic (BRANCH-B-E007) attached to the routing\n72\tinvestigation. Critical path length: 2.\n73\t\n74\t## Dependency DAG\n75\t\n76\t```mermaid\n77\tgraph TD\n78\t    E001[E001 Investigation: workspace provider init coupling] --> E005[E005 Implementation: plugin workspace resolution]\n79\t    E002[E002 Investigation: path resolvability / enforcement completeness] --> E004[E004 Implementation: workspace-aware artifacts-only serving]\n80\t    E003[E003 Investigation: artifact traffic routing design] --> E006[E006 Implementation: operator split-server topology]\n81\t    E003 -.-> E007B[BRANCH-B-E007 Implementation: server-side delegation config]\n82\t```\n83\t\n84\tArrows point from dependency to dependent. The dotted edge selects a conditional branch epic, which\n85\tis not a main-plan node. All three investigations are parallel roots; the three implementations are\n86\tparallel once their gating investigation resolves.\n87\t\n88\t## DAG Justification\n89\t\n90\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n91\t|---|---|---|\n92\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates Implementation) | Open question \"Does the workspace store initialization in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins depend on tracking store availability?\" explicitly \"Determines scope of plugin refactoring needed for the artifacts-only context\" — the answer rewrites the plugin epic's approach (true gate, `rewrite`). |\n93\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 3 + Rule 19 | The assumption \"artifact request paths ... include sufficient information to resolve workspace membership\" is flagged \"needs validation\", and risks 1 (path resolution complexity) and 3 (bypass via misconfiguration) leave the enforcement surface unverified. The answer changes the server epic's resolution/validation approach (true gate, `rewrite`). |\n94\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 3 | Open question \"How should the operator configure artifact traffic routing ...?\" explicitly \"Affects HTTPRoute design and determines whether the tracking server needs a configuration change\" — the answer rewrites the operator epic's routing scope (true gate, `rewrite`). |\n95\t| E003 → BRANCH-B-E007 | Rule 3 (conditional branch) | Bounded outcome B (tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations) adds a server-side epic that exists only in that branch. |\n96\t\n97\t**Rule 19 handling (safety-critical: tenant isolation / access control).** The strategy introduces new\n98\tauthorization enforcement (workspace validation of artifact requests with fail-closed denial), so\n99\tRule 19 applies. The fail-mode decision itself, however, is already fixed by the strategy (fail-closed\n100\tis HLR P0-3, the Security NFR, and acceptance criterion 4), so a standalone fail-mode investigation\n101\twould not change what gets built (Rule 4). The residual fail-mode unknown (coverage of unresolvable\n102\tpaths, plugin errors, malformed workspace context) and the security unknown (bypass vectors via\n103\tnon-standard artifact roots, misconfiguration) are resolved by the same analysis of the same\n104\tvalidation path, so they are combined into E002 per Step 3's combine rule rather than split into two\n105\tinvestigations.\n106\t\n107\t**Edges deliberately NOT drawn:**\n108\t\n109\t| Non-edge | Rule | Rationale |\n110\t|---|---|---|\n111\t| Server implementation → operator implementation | Rule 13 (configuration reference) | The operator deploys the MLflow image by configuration reference, not a build-against artifact. The operator epic carries a \"deployed image supports the combined flags\" AC instead of a DAG edge, so operator and server development run in parallel. |\n112\t| Plugin implementation → server implementation | Rules 13/14 | The server delegates to the existing auth plugin interface (`mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point / `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`), which is unchanged; the plugin epic changes plugin internals behind that interface. Different repos, pre-existing interface → parallel; end-to-end behavior is gated by ACs on both epics. |\n113\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (scoping) | The strategy's risk mitigation confines the coupling fix to the plugin (\"factor workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin\"); the server epic's provider interface is stable, so the investigation gates the plugin epic only. |\n114\t| No konflux/repo onboarding chain | Rule 20 (not triggered) | All three components already exist in the platform; no new component is provisioned. |\n115\t\n116\t**Rule 4 reclassifications (unknowns that do NOT change epic structure):**\n117\t\n118\t| Open question / assumption | Handling |\n119\t|---|---|\n120\t| Acceptable latency overhead for workspace validation (PM/Engineering) | AC on the server epic: measure per-request overhead; implement short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions per the strategy's risk mitigation. Does not change which epics exist. Health warning: no latency target is specified. |\n121\t| Audit telemetry channel for denials (PM/Security) | HLR P2 already commits to server logs; implemented as denial-path logging in the server epic. Channel selection (OTLP / Kubernetes audit log) is a follow-up extension, not a structural change. |\n122\t| Flags composable without breaking individual-flag init sequences | This is the server epic's own deliverable (its ACs cover individual-flag backwards compatibility), not a separate investigation. |\n123\t| Operator Helm chart supports a second Deployment without structural changes | Implementation detail of the operator epic; AC covers the rendered resources. |\n124\t| Single-tenant deployments do not set `--enable-workspaces` | Regression AC on the server epic (current behavior preserved when workspace flags are absent). |\n125\t\n126\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n127\t\n128\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n129\t|---|---|---|\n130\t| `--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` to enable workspace-validated artifact serving | P0 | E004 |\n131\t| Artifact requests through the workspace-aware artifacts-only server are validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | P0 | E004 (enforcement), E005 (resolution capability) |\n132\t| Workspace-aware artifact serving is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable, requests are denied | P0 | E004 (E002 verifies coverage) |\n133\t| Single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior with no changes | P0 | E004, E006 |\n134\t| MLflow operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute | P1 | E006 |\n135\t| Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance | P1 | E006, BRANCH-B-E007 (branch B only) |\n136\t| Auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization | P1 | E005 |\n137\t| Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs | P2 | E004 |\n138\t\n139\tAll 8 HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements. The P2 audit-logging HLR stays bundled in E004: it is\n140\tthe same denial code path and shares the same surface as the P0 validation work — a deferral split\n141\twould create two epics building the same validation path (Step 4 bundling rule for tightly coupled\n142\tlower-priority HLRs).\n143\t\n144\t## Priority Inheritance Notes\n145\t\n146\t- E005 maps to a P1 HLR but transitively enables the P0 workspace-membership validation HLR →\n147\t  inherits P0.\n148\t- E001 and E002 block P0 work → P0. E003 gates only P1 work → P1.\n149\t- BRANCH-B-E007 enables only the P1 artifact-traffic-routing HLR → P1.\n150\t\n151\t## Tiered Delivery\n152\t\n153\tNot applicable. All dependencies are internal and existing (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway\n154\tAPI, kube-rbac-proxy); there is no external dependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1/Tier 2\n155\tsplit is needed.\n156\t\n157\t## Conditional Branches\n158\t\n159\tThe routing-design investigation (E003) has two bounded outcomes:\n160\t\n161\t- **Outcome A — HTTPRoute-only routing suffices**: Gateway API HTTPRoute rules direct artifact\n162\t  traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance; no tracking server code change. Main-plan E006\n163\t  covers this alone; no branch epic exists.\n164\t- **Outcome B — server-side delegation required**: the tracking server needs a configuration change\n165\t  to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated instance. BRANCH-B-E007 implements the\n166\t  server-side delegation configuration; it is attached to the investigation as a plan document and\n167\t  is created only if outcome B holds.\n168\t\n169\t## Health Warnings\n170\t\n171\t1. **Priority label vs. enablement**: the strategy labels the plugin resolution work P1 (HLR), but\n172\t   the P0 workspace-validation HLR cannot function without it; priority inheritance raises the\n173\t   plugin epic to P0.\n174\t2. **No latency target**: the Performance NFR admits no latency target is specified; whether caching\n175\t   is required at launch is an open PM/Engineering decision. Mitigated by an AC to measure overhead\n176\t   and implement short-TTL caching consistent with existing auth-path caching.\n177\t3. **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins lacks a dedicated architecture-context file**: the plugin epic works\n178\t   from mlflow.md's description of the entry-point interface; the source repo location should be\n179\t   confirmed at epic start (repo_access scored neutral).\n180\t4. **Strategy-level process gates pending**: Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, Licence\n181\t   Validation and other prerequisites are \"Pending human review\"; they are strategy-level gates and\n182\t   are not modeled as epics.\n183\t5. **Non-standard artifact roots**: the initial implementation is scoped to the S3 artifact store\n184\t   (`{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`); limitations for custom artifact roots must be documented\n185\t   (risk 1 mitigation) — a documented restriction, not a scope expansion.\n186\t6. **Split-topology misconfiguration risk**: a split server adds operational surface where\n187\t   misconfiguration could let artifact requests bypass workspace validation; the operator epic must\n188\t   reject split-serving configurations whose artifacts-only instance lacks workspace flags\n189\t   (risk 3 mitigation).\n190\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d0bd6bd2abb5543","agent_id":"a0d0c460e417dbaec","session_id":"7838be6a-6dd6-47c0-b294-a71fb9a59c49","uuid":"b13c49bc-9dce-48e7-b457-7a1b2ed4d61e","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:23:01.776Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8fb