{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9dc94c47acac4939","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tscore: 11\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: major\n8\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n9\t  description: 'E006 bundles the entire operator split-deployment workstream — which\n10\t    the strategy estimates at 2-3 sprints — into one epic: new Helm templates for\n11\t    Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute, workspace config propagation, artifact traffic routing,\n12\t    declarative CR surface, misconfiguration validation, sidecar, and backwards-compat\n13\t    reconciliation. That is multiple sprints of work, 2-3x the ~2-week epic guideline\n14\t    and 2-3x the sibling plugin epic the strategy prices at 1 sprint. Split along\n15\t    the deliverable axis: (a) deploy the dedicated artifacts-only instance (templates,\n16\t    config propagation, kube-rbac-proxy sidecar, misconfiguration validation, backwards\n17\t    compat) and (b) artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment — the\n18\t    E003-gated part that pairs with BRANCH-B-E007 under outcome B.'\n19\t- severity: minor\n20\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n21\t  description: 'E004 is borderline oversized: the strategy estimates server-side flag\n22\t    composition and workspace validation at 1-2 sprints, and the epic also carries\n23\t    audit logging, short-TTL caching, and non-standard-artifact-root limitation documentation.\n24\t    Re-check after the E006 split; not itself a violation at the low end of the estimate.'\n25\t- severity: minor\n26\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n27\t  description: BRANCH-B-E007 scores pattern_precedent=0 while its own rationale states\n28\t    the rubric -1 condition verbatim (no existing mechanism in the tracking server\n29\t    for delegating artifact operations to another instance); change_specificity=0\n30\t    is similarly arguable for -1 given the delegation mechanism is unknown until E003\n31\t    resolves.\n32\terror: null\n33\t---\n34\t## Review Summary\n35\t\n36\tScore: 11/14 — fail\n37\tRecommendation: revise\n38\t\n39\tContract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py` exits 0 — body sections, canonical\n40\tcomponent names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, and priority inheritance all pass). Triage\n41\tconsistency: `triage` is null (full decomposition), so the 6-epic + 1-branch shape is expected.\n42\tThe decomposition is structurally sound — DAG, gating, HLR traceability, and signal hygiene are\n43\tall clean — and fails on a single dimension: E006 is an oversized epic (Criterion 3), packing\n44\t2-3 sprints of operator work into one planning unit.\n45\t\n46\t## Criterion Details\n47\t\n48\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n49\tAll 8 HLRs (4x P0, 3x P1, 1x P2) map to at least one epic via a present traceability matrix,\n50\tand each epic's HLR Traceability section is consistent with the matrix. Priority inheritance is\n51\tcorrect: E001/E002 (P0) gate P0 implementations; E003 (P1) gates only P1 work (E006,\n52\tBRANCH-B-E007); E005 correctly inherits P0 through enablement of the P0 workspace-validation\n53\tHLR. No lower-priority epic blocks P0 work. Priority collapse: the P2 audit-logging HLR is\n54\tbundled into E004, but it is the same denial code path as the P0 validation work (incidental,\n55\tnot an independently deferrable feature) — justified, not a collapse. E006's P0 \"regression\n56\tshare\" does not make it a P0 blocker of anything.\n57\t\n58\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n59\tNo cycles; every edge is a justified Rule 3 investigation→implementation gate grounded in a\n60\tstrategy open question or \"needs validation\" assumption. Diagram and frontmatter agree in both\n61\tdirections (E001→E005, E002→E004, E003→E006, E003⇢BRANCH-B-E007), arrows correctly drawn\n62\tdependency→dependent. The branch file sets both `branch: B` and `gated_by: E003`, and no\n63\tmain-plan epic lists the branch epic in `dependencies`. No konflux/repo-onboarding epics exist,\n64\tso rules 7/8/10 cannot be violated. The three deliberately-not-drawn edges are correctly\n65\thandled: server→operator is a configuration reference (image capability) carried as an AC gate\n66\ton E006 per Rule 13, not a DAG edge; plugin→server goes through a pre-existing interface\n67\t(Rules 13/14); E001 gates only the plugin epic per the strategy's own risk mitigation. No\n68\tdata/artifact dependency discoverable in epic content is missing from the DAG. Critical path\n69\tlength 2 is at the low end for an M-L strategy, but that reflects genuinely parallel structure\n70\t(all components exist, no onboarding or build-against chains) and the rubric penalizes only\n71\tover-serialization — noted as an observation, not a defect.\n72\t\n73\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (0/2)\n74\tAll epics are one team (MLflow Team) and one canonical component (MLflow), with the\n75\tnon-canonical `MLflow Operator` / `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` strategy components correctly\n76\tmapped and documented; the validator confirms canonical names. No scope duplication between\n77\tE004 (server validation) and E005 (plugin resolution) — complementary layers in different\n78\trepos — and no work-product type mixing. The defect is size: the strategy's own effort\n79\testimate prices the operator split-deployment topology at **2-3 sprints**, and E006's scope is\n80\texactly that workstream (new Helm templates for Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute, workspace config\n81\tpropagation, artifact traffic routing, declarative CR surface, misconfiguration validation,\n82\tsidecar, backwards-compat reconciliation). That is multiple sprints of work in one epic —\n83\t2-3x the ~2-week guideline and 2-3x the sibling plugin epic the strategy prices at 1 sprint.\n84\tIt is clearly separable along the deliverable axis: (a) deploy the artifacts-only instance\n85\t(templates, config propagation, sidecar, validation, backwards compat) and (b) artifact\n86\ttraffic routing from the main tracking deployment — the E003-gated part that pairs with\n87\tBRANCH-B-E007 under outcome B. E004 (strategy estimate 1-2 sprints, plus audit logging,\n88\tcaching, and limitations documentation) is borderline and should be re-checked after the E006\n89\tsplit, but is not itself a violation at the low end of its estimate.\n90\t\n91\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n92\tAll three Investigations genuinely change downstream structure: E001's outcome rewrites E005's\n93\tscope (verify vs. refactor), E002's outcome rewrites E004's resolution/validation approach,\n94\tE003's outcome selects E006's routing scope and whether BRANCH-B-E007 exists at all. All\n95\tImplementations produce concrete artifacts. Gating metadata is complete and correct: every\n96\tgated epic has `gated_by` set to an Investigation that appears in its direct `dependencies`,\n97\tand every `gate_failure_impact` has both `action` and a substantive `fallback_approach`\n98\t(rewrite for E004/E005/E006; remove for the branch epic, correctly reflecting outcome A).\n99\t\n100\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n101\tType-appropriate signal sets are used throughout (investigation_signals on E001-E003,\n102\tai_signals on the four Implementations); no epic mixes sets, and all seven rationale files\n103\texist. Signal values are consistent with rubric conditions and strategy content, including the\n104\thonest negatives (E005 repo_access=0 with unconfirmed plugin repo, matching Health Warning 3;\n105\tE004/E005/E006 open_questions=-1 reflecting their gates). One arguable mis-score:\n106\tBRANCH-B-E007 sets `pattern_precedent: 0` while its own rationale states the rubric's -1\n107\tcondition verbatim (\"The tracking server has no existing mechanism for delegating artifact\n108\toperations to another instance\"); `change_specificity: 0` is similarly arguable for -1 given\n109\tthe delegation mechanism is unknown until E003 resolves. One-to-two arguable values → 1 point,\n110\tnot a rubric cont