{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-afb65e53b189539f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tepic_count: 7\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8009 — Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n11\t\n12\t## Triage\n13\t\n14\tFull decomposition (not triaged to a single epic). The strategy is M-L effort (4-7 sprints), spans\n15\tthree affected components (MLflow, MLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins) with code changes in\n16\teach, and carries strategy-flagged open questions and \"needs validation\" assumptions whose answers\n17\tchange downstream epic scope. Not docs-only: every affected component requires code changes.\n18\t\n19\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n20\t\n21\tAll three Staff Engineer / SME corrections are incorporated; the strategy's technical approach already\n22\treflects them, and epic scope follows the corrected direction:\n23\t\n24\t| # | Staff Engineer Input | Effect on decomposition |\n25\t|---|---------------------|-------------------------|\n26\t| 1 | No new flag — combine `--artifacts-only` with the existing `--enable-workspaces` | No epic creates `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` or `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware`; the server implementation epic composes existing flags only |\n27\t| 2 | Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle | The operator work is scoped as two epics — a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute (E006) and artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment (E008) — not a CR-field toggle |\n28\t| 3 | SSAR detail is over-specified (plugin implementation detail, not strategy-level) | Epic scope states that authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure without dictating Kubernetes API mechanics; kube-rbac-proxy / `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup unchanged |\n29\t\n30\t## Component Graph\n31\t\n32\t| Strategy component | Change | Owner team | Canonical Jira component | Active/Passive |\n33\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n34\t| MLflow | `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces`; workspace provider init without tracking/registry stores; workspace validation of artifact requests | MLflow Team | MLflow | Active |\n35\t| MLflow Operator | Dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute; artifact traffic routing from main tracking deployment | MLflow Team | MLflow (mapping) | Active |\n36\t| mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | Verify and extend workspace resolution for the artifacts-only context | MLflow Team | MLflow (mapping) | Active |\n37\t\n38\t**Component name mappings (non-obvious):** `.context/rhai-components.txt` has no `MLflow Operator`\n39\tor `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` entry. Both map to the canonical `MLflow` component — the closest,\n40\tmost-relevant canonical name (submit.py strips non-canonical values, leaving the Jira epic\n41\tcomponent-less). Each epic carries a single canonical component; the strategy-component span is\n42\tnoted here.\n43\t\n44\t**New component detection:** MLflow and MLflow Operator have architecture context files (mlflow.md,\n45\tmlflow-operator.md). mlflow-kubernetes-plugins has no dedicated architecture-context file, but it is\n46\tan existing internal component (v1.5.0, installed into the MLflow image and documented in mlflow.md;\n47\tthe strategy's dependency table marks it \"internal, exists\") — it is not a new component being\n48\tprovisioned, so no Rule 20 repo/konflux onboarding chain is generated. See Health Warnings.\n49\t\n50\t**Passive components (validation folded into ACs, same team):**\n51\t\n52\t- **kube-rbac-proxy** — unchanged; deployed as a sidecar on the artifacts-only instance identically\n53\t  to the main tracking deployment → AC on the operator epic.\n54\t- **Gateway API** — existing conditional dependency with runtime CRD discovery → scope of the\n55\t  operator epic.\n56\t- **Workspace store (`kubernetes://`)** — exists; unreachable-store behavior is fail-closed → ACs on\n57\t  the server epic.\n58\t\n59\t## Epic List\n60\t\n61\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n62\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n63\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Investigate workspace provider initialization coupling in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n64\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Investigate artifact path workspace-context resolvability and enforcement completeness | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n65\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Investigate artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n66\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n67\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Extend mlflow-kubernetes-plugins workspace resolution for the artifacts-only context | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n68\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | MLflow operator deployment of a dedicated artifacts-only instance | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n69\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E008 | MLflow operator artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n70\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007 | Tracking server artifact traffic delegation configuration (branch B) | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n71\t\n72\tMain-plan epics: 7 (E001-E006, E008 — the E007 sequence number is taken by the conditional branch\n73\tepic). One conditional branch epic (BRANCH-B-E007) attached to the routing investigation. Critical\n74\tpath length: 2.\n75\t\n76\tRevision note (post-review): the original single operator epic covering the whole split-deployment\n77\tworkstream was split along the deliverable axis per the adversarial review — E006 now deploys the\n78\tdedicated artifacts-only instance (templates, workspace config propagation, kube-rbac-proxy sidecar,\n79\tmisconfiguration validation, backwards compat), and E008 carries artifact traffic routing from the\n80\tmain tracking deployment, the routing-investigation-gated part that pairs with BRANCH-B-E007 under\n81\toutcome B.\n82\t\n83\t## Dependency DAG\n84\t\n85\t```mermaid\n86\tgraph TD\n87\t    E001[E001 Investigation: workspace provider init coupling] --> E005[E005 Implementation: plugin workspace resolution]\n88\t    E002[E002 Investigation: path resolvability / enforcement completeness] --> E004[E004 Implementation: workspace-aware artifacts-only serving]\n89\t    E003[E003 Investigation: artifact traffic routing design] --> E006[E006 Implementation: operator deployment of artifacts-only instance]\n90\t    E003 --> E008[E008 Implementation: operator artifact traffic routing]\n91\t    E003 -.-> E007B[BRANCH-B-E007 Implementation: server-side delegation config]\n92\t```\n93\t\n94\tArrows point from dependency to dependent. The dotted edge selects a conditional branch epic, which\n95\tis not a main-plan node. All three investigations are parallel roots; the implementations are\n96\tparallel once their gating investigation resolves.\n97\t\n98\t## DAG Justification\n99\t\n100\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n101\t|---|---|---|\n102\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates Implementation) | Open question \"Does the workspace store initialization in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins depend on tracking store availability?\" explicitly \"Determines scope of plugin refactoring needed for the artifacts-only context\" — the answer rewrites the plugin epic's approach (true gate, `rewrite`). |\n103\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 3 + Rule 19 | The assumption \"artifact request paths ... include sufficient information to resolve workspace membership\" is flagged \"needs validation\", and risks 1 (path resolution complexity) and 3 (bypass via misconfiguration) leave the enforcement surface unverified. The answer changes the server epic's resolution/validation approach (true gate, `rewrite`). |\n104\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 3 | Open question \"How should the operator configure artifact traffic routing ...?\" explicitly \"Affects HTTPRoute design\" — the answer may change the artifacts-only instance's HTTPRoute design within the deployment epic (true gate, `rewrite`; the deployment templates, flag propagation, sidecar, and validation scope are gate-independent). |\n105\t| E003 → E008 | Rule 3 | The same open question \"determines whether the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations\" — the answer selects this epic's routing mechanism (HTTPRoute rules vs operator-side delegation propagation) and rewrites its scope (true gate, `rewrite`). |\n106\t| E003 → BRANCH-B-E007 | Rule 3 (conditional branch) | Bounded outcome B (tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations) adds a server-side epic that exists only in that branch. |\n107\t\n108\t**Rule 19 handling (safety-critical: tenant isolation / access control).** The strategy introduces new\n109\tauthorization enforcement (workspace validation of artifact requests with fail-closed denial), so\n110\tRule 19 applies. The fail-mode decision itself, however, is already fixed by the strategy (fail-closed\n111\tis HLR P0-3, the Security NFR, and acceptance criterion 4), so a standalone fail-mode investigation\n112\twould not change what gets built (Rule 4). The residual fail-mode unknown (coverage of unresolvable\n113\tpaths, plugin errors, malformed workspace context) and the security unknown (bypass vectors via\n114\tnon-standard artifact roots, misconfiguration) are resolved by the same analysis of the same\n115\tvalidation path, so they are combined into E002 per Step 3's combine rule rather than split into two\n116\tinvestigations.\n117\t\n118\t**Edges deliberately NOT drawn:**\n119\t\n120\t| Non-edge | Rule | Rationale |\n121\t|---|---|---|\n122\t| Server implementation → operator implementation | Rule 13 (configuration reference) | The operator deploys the MLflow image by configuration reference, not a build-against artifact. The operator epic carries a \"deployed image supports the combined flags\" AC instead of a DAG edge, so operator and server development run in parallel. |\n123\t| Operator deployment epic → operator routing epic | Rule 13 (configuration reference) | Both are rendered by the same MLflow CR controller from the same embedded chart: the routing configuration targets the artifacts-only instance's Service by configuration reference, not a build-against artifact. The two operator sub-deliverables develop in parallel and integrate in one reconciliation. |\n124\t| Plugin implementation → server implementation | Rules 13/14 | The server delegates to the existing auth plugin interface (`mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point / `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`), which is unchanged; the plugin epic changes plugin internals behind that interface. Different repos, pre-existing interface → parallel; end-to-end behavior is gated by ACs on both epics. |\n125\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (scoping) | The strategy's risk mitigation confines the coupling fix to the plugin (\"factor workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin\"); the server epic's provider interface is stable, so the investigation gates the plugin epic only. |\n126\t| No konflux/repo onboarding chain | Rule 20 (not triggered) | All three components already exist in the platform; no new component is provisioned. |\n127\t\n128\t**Rule 4 reclassifications (unknowns that do NOT change epic structure):**\n129\t\n130\t| Open question / assumption | Handling |\n131\t|---|---|\n132\t| Acceptable latency overhead for workspace validation (PM/Engineering) | AC on the server epic: measure per-request overhead; implement short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions per the strategy's risk mitigation. Does not change which epics exist. Health warning: no latency target is specified. |\n133\t| Audit telemetry channel for denials (PM/Security) | HLR P2 already commits to server logs; implemented as denial-path logging in the server epic. Channel selection (OTLP / Kubernetes audit log) is a follow-up extension, not a structural change. |\n134\t| Flags composable without breaking individual-flag init sequences | This is the server epic's own deliverable (its ACs cover individual-flag backwards compatibility), not a separate investigation. |\n135\t| Operator Helm chart supports a second Deployment without structural changes | Implementation detail of the operator epic; AC covers the rendered resources. |\n136\t| Single-tenant deployments do not set `--enable-workspaces` | Regression AC on the server epic (current behavior preserved when workspace flags are absent). |\n137\t\n138\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n139\t\n140\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n141\t|---|---|---|\n142\t| `--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` to enable workspace-validated artifact serving | P0 | E004 |\n143\t| Artifact requests through the workspace-aware artifacts-only server are validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | P0 | E004 (enforcement), E005 (resolution capability) |\n144\t| Workspace-aware artifact serving is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable, requests are denied | P0 | E004 (E002 verifies coverage) |\n145\t| Single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior with no changes | P0 | E004, E006, E008 |\n146\t| MLflow operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute | P1 | E006 |\n147\t| Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance | P1 | E008, BRANCH-B-E007 (branch B only) |\n148\t| Auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization | P1 | E005 |\n149\t| Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs | P2 | E004 |\n150\t\n151\tAll 8 HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements. The P2 audit-logging HLR stays bundled in E004: it is\n152\tthe same denial code path and shares the same surface as the P0 validation work — a deferral split\n153\twould create two epics building the same validation path (Step 4 bundling rule for tightly coupled\n154\tlower-priority HLRs).\n155\t\n156\t## Priority Inheritance Notes\n157\t\n158\t- E005 maps to a P1 HLR but transitively enables the P0 workspace-membership validation HLR →\n159\t  inherits P0.\n160\t- E001 and E002 block P0 work → P0. E003 gates only P1 work → P1.\n161\t- BRANCH-B-E007 enables only the P1 artifact-traffic-routing HLR → P1.\n162\t\n163\t## Tiered Delivery\n164\t\n165\tNot applicable. All dependencies are internal and existing (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway\n166\tAPI, kube-rbac-proxy); there is no external dependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1/Tier 2\n167\tsplit is needed.\n168\t\n169\t## Conditional Branches\n170\t\n171\tThe routing-design investigation (E003) has two bounded outcomes:\n172\t\n173\t- **Outcome A — HTTPRoute-only routing suffices**: Gateway API HTTPRoute rules direct artifact\n174\t  traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance; no tracking server code change. Main-plan E008\n175\t  implements the HTTPRoute-based routing; no branch epic exists.\n176\t- **Outcome B — server-side delegation required**: the tracking server needs a configuration change\n177\t  to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated instance. E008 implements the operator-side\n178\t  routing/delegation propagation and pairs with BRANCH-B-E007, which implements the server-side\n179\t  delegation configuration; the branch epic is attached to the investigation as a plan document and\n180\t  is created only if outcome B holds.\n181\t\n182\t## Health Warnings\n183\t\n184\t1. **Priority label vs. enablement**: the strategy labels the plugin resolution work P1 (HLR), but\n185\t   the P0 workspace-validation HLR cannot function without it; priority inheritance raises the\n186\t   plugin epic to P0.\n187\t2. **No latency target**: the Performance NFR admits no latency target is specified; whether caching\n188\t   is required at launch is an open PM/Engineering decision. Mitigated by an AC to measure overhead\n189\t   and implement short-TTL caching consistent with existing auth-path caching.\n190\t3. **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins lacks a dedicated architecture-context file**: the plugin epic works\n191\t   from mlflow.md's description of the entry-point interface; the source repo location should be\n192\t   confirmed at epic start (repo_access scored neutral).\n193\t4. **Strategy-level process gates pending**: Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, Licence\n194\t   Validation and other prerequisites are \"Pending human review\"; they are strategy-level gates and\n195\t   are not modeled as epics.\n196\t5. **Non-standard artifact roots**: the initial implementation is scoped to the S3 artifact store\n197\t   (`{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`); limitations for custom artifact roots must be documented\n198\t   (risk 1 mitigation) — a documented restriction, not a scope expansion.\n199\t6. **Split-topology misconfiguration risk**: a split server adds operational surface where\n200\t   misconfiguration could let artifact requests bypass workspace validation; the operator epic must\n201\t   reject split-serving configurations whose artifacts-only instance lacks workspace flags\n202\t   (risk 3 mitigation).\n203\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a3abadb3cf21788a","agent_id":"ac79b6f5c19b332c7","session_id":"7838be6a-6dd6-47c0-b294-a71fb9a59c49","uuid":"2fead5ef-c3ed-433d-89f2-57cb0fc75516","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:35:55.395Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-afb65e53b189539f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
