{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-946d30385d63060a","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E004\n3\ttitle: Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n5\tcomponent: MLflow\n6\tteam: MLflow Team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E002\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E002\n12\tgate_failure_impact:\n13\t  action: rewrite\n14\t  fallback_approach: If artifact request paths prove insufficient to resolve workspace\n15\t    membership, or uncovered bypass/failure modes are found, the resolution and validation\n16\t    approach of this epic changes and remediation work is added per the investigation\n17\t    findings.\n18\tai_signals:\n19\t  change_specificity: 1\n20\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n21\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n22\t  existing_foundation: 1\n23\t  open_questions: -1\n24\t  external_dependency: 0\n25\t  human_process_gates: 0\n26\t  repo_access: 1\n27\t  architecture_claims: 1\n28\timplementation_type: null\n29\tinvestigation_signals: null\n30\tai_implementability: High\n31\tai_implementability_score: 5\n32\tjira_key: null\n33\tbranch: null\n34\t---\n35\t# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n36\t\n37\t## Description\n38\t\n39\tMake `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` in the MLflow tracking server so the\n40\tartifacts-only server initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests\n41\tagainst tenant boundaries. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit\n42\t`--workspace-store-uri`, resolves each artifact request's path to its parent experiment and the\n43\texperiment's workspace assignment, and delegates authorization to the existing auth plugin\n44\tinfrastructure. No new server flag is introduced (Staff Engineer input: the workspace-aware behavior\n45\temerges from combining existing flags). Tracking stores and model registry stores stay\n46\tuninitialized, and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n47\t\n48\t## Scope\n49\t\n50\t- Flag composition: allow `--artifacts-only` to combine with `--enable-workspaces`; require an\n51\t  explicit `--workspace-store-uri` in combined mode and fail startup with a clear error when it is\n52\t  missing.\n53\t- Initialize only the workspace provider needed for artifact validation, without tracking store or\n54\t  model registry store initialization; keep non-artifact API endpoints disabled.\n55\t- Workspace validation of artifact requests: resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment\n56\t  (S3 artifact store paths follow `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), look up the experiment's\n57\t  workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing auth\n58\t  plugin infrastructure — without prescribing Kubernetes API mechanics inside the plugin (Staff\n59\t  Engineer input: the plugin's authorization mechanics are an implementation detail, not\n60\t  strategy-level).\n61\t- Fail-closed behavior: if workspace-aware mode is active but the workspace store is unreachable,\n62\t  deny artifact requests (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable) rather than\n63\t  serving without validation; requests with invalid, missing, or unresolvable workspace context are\n64\t  denied, not served.\n65\t- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` retains current\n66\t  behavior (artifacts served without workspace validation); each flag used individually preserves\n67\t  its current initialization sequence; no existing CLI arguments, API contracts, or configuration\n68\t  keys change meaning.\n69\t- Audit logging: workspace access denials in artifact serving are logged through server logs.\n70\t- Performance: measure per-request overhead of workspace resolution; implement short-TTL caching of\n71\t  workspace membership decisions consistent with caching patterns already used in the tracking\n72\t  server's auth path (the strategy sets no latency target — the acceptability threshold is an open\n73\t  PM/Engineering decision).\n74\t- Document limitations for non-standard artifact roots (the initial implementation is scoped to the\n75\t  S3 artifact store per the strategy's risk mitigation).\n76\t\n77\t## Acceptance Criteria\n78\t\n79\t- Given the server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri\n80\t  kubernetes://`, a user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact belonging to an\n81\t  experiment in workspace A receives HTTP 200 with the correct artifact content.\n82\t- Given the same deployment, a user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an\n83\t  artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B receives HTTP 403 with an error message\n84\t  indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n85\t- Given the server started with `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces`, any authenticated\n86\t  user's artifact request is served without workspace validation (HTTP 200 regardless of workspace\n87\t  membership) — current behavior preserved.\n88\t- Given the workspace-aware artifacts-only server running with the workspace store (Kubernetes API)\n89\t  unreachable, artifact requests are denied with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is\n90\t  unavailable (fail-closed).\n91\t- Artifact requests with invalid, missing, or unresolvable workspace context are denied, not served.\n92\t- Starting the server with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` but no `--workspace-store-uri`\n93\t  fails to start with a clear error message.\n94\t- In combined mode, tracking stores and model registry stores remain uninitialized and non-artifact\n95\t  API endpoints remain disabled.\n96\t- Workspace access denials in artifact serving appear in server logs.\n97\t- Per-request latency overhead of workspace resolution is measured and reported, and short-TTL\n98\t  caching of workspace membership decisions is implemented consistent with the tracking server's\n99\t  existing auth-path caching.\n100\t- Existing behavior for each flag used individually is unchanged (regression: single-flag and\n101\t  no-flag startup sequences and artifact serving behave as before).\n102\t\n103\t## HLR Traceability\n104\t\n105\t- P0: `--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` to\n106\t  enable workspace-validated artifact serving.\n107\t- P0: Artifact requests through the workspace-aware artifacts-only server are validated against\n108\t  workspace membership; requests from users without workspace membership are denied with HTTP 403.\n109\t- P0: Workspace-aware artifact serving is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable,\n110\t  requests are denied.\n111\t- P0: Single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current\n112\t  behavior with no changes.\n113\t- P2: Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs (bundled:\n114\t  the same denial code path and surface as the P0 validation work — not an independently deferrable\n115\t  feature).\n116\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a3abadb3cf21788a","agent_id":"ac79b6f5c19b332c7","session_id":"7838be6a-6dd6-47c0-b294-a71fb9a59c49","uuid":"2071cc5a-2fac-4a8b-ab5d-563f106d8e56","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:36:03.922Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-946d30385d63060a","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
